{% extends 'Base/base.html.twig' %}

{% import 'Shared/extra.html.twig' as extra %}

{% block body %}
    {{ extra.breadcrumb('GHDI Extra', 'teaching-index', true) }}

    <h1>{{ entry.title[app.request.locale] }}</h1>
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-sm-12">
            <p><em>Lesson Plan for intermediate and advanced collegiate courses in
                    history.</em></p>
            <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
            <p>This lesson plan and its accompanying materials are designed to
                introduce college students to the ways in which historians use digital
                mapmaking and GIS (geographic information systems) technology to better
                understand historical events. It provides students with a basic
                technical introduction to QGIS, a GIS software package commonly used in
                the digital humanities and spatial history projects. In a hands-on
                exercise, students will use QGIS to map centers of Protestant and
                Catholic printing in Reformation-Era Germany, using data visualization
                techniques to better understand how print technology contributed to the
                spread of religious ideas.</p>
            <h2 id="assigned-reading-and-preparation">Assigned Reading and
                Preparation</h2>
            <p>Before class, students should read Chapter 1 from Marc Edwards Jr.’s
                <em><a
                        href="https://publishing.cdlib.org/ucpressebooks/view?docId=ft3q2nb278&amp;chunk.id=d0e303&amp;toc.depth=1&amp;toc.id=d0e303&amp;brand=ucpress"
                    ><u>Printing,
                            Propaganda, and Martin Luther</u></a></em> (University of California
                Press, 1994)<em>.</em> They should also look at the maps of <a
                    href="https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/from-the-reformations-to-the-thirty-years-war-1500-1648/centers-of-catholic-and-protestant-printing-16th-century"
                ><u>Protestant
                        and Catholic Printing</u></a> in GHDI Volume 1.</p>
            <p>Students should also complete the following steps so that they will
                be able to use QGIS for the in-class assignment.<a
                    href="#fn1"
                    class="footnote-ref"
                    id="fnref1"
                    role="doc-noteref"
                ><sup>1</sup></a></p>
            <ol class="incremental" type="1">
                <li><p>Download and install QGIS on their laptops from <a
                            href="https://qgis.org/download/"
                        ><u>https://qgis.org/download/</u></a>
                        . Students should download the most up-to-date Long Term Release (LTR)
                        of the software. Students are encouraged to “click around” the program
                        on their own before class to build initial familiarity with its tools
                        and layout.</p></li>
                <li><p>Download the <strong><a href="https://germanhistorydocs.org/media/extra/teaching-exercise-materials-using-gis-to-map-protestant-and-catholic-printing.zip">Exercise Materials</a></strong> [ZIP] folder included
                        with this course. Students will need to unzip the folder and save its
                        contents in an easy-to-find location on their computer.</p></li>
            </ol>
            <h2
                id="exercise-using-gis-to-map-protestant-and-catholic-printing"
            >Exercise:
                Using GIS to Map Protestant and Catholic Printing</h2>
            <p>Students should follow along with the <strong><a href="https://germanhistorydocs.org/media/extra/teaching-exercise-instructions-using-gis-to-map-protestant-and-catholic-printing.pdf">Exercise
                    Instructions</a></strong> [PDF]. Since students will work at different paces, it
                is recommended to let them work independently, and for the instructor(s)
                to circulate around the classroom providing support as necessary.</p>
            <p>At the end of the exercise, reconvene as a class for a discussion on
                the following questions.</p>
            <ol class="incremental" type="1">
                <li><p>Are there noteworthy trends in your data about the relative scale
                        and geographic distribution of Protestant and Catholic printing in the
                        early decades of the Reformation? What historical arguments might this
                        data support?</p></li>
                <li><p>Can you think of other ways in which GIS technology can assist
                        historians, and other types of historical questions it can help answer?
                        How might you use this technology to explore your own research
                        interests?</p></li>
            </ol>
            <h2 id="further-resources"><strong>Further Resources</strong></h2>
            <p>QGIS offers a detailed <a
                    href="https://docs.qgis.org/3.44/en/docs/training_manual/index.html"
                ><u>Training
                        Manual</u></a>, as well as <a
                    href="https://docs.qgis.org/3.44/en/docs/gentle_gis_introduction/index.html"
                ><u>A
                        Gentle Introduction to GIS</u></a>, both of which provide further
                information and resources for students interested in expanding their GIS
                research skills.</p>
            <p>Other helpful resources, particularly for historical GIS (HGIS) work,
                include the following. Note that natively-online resources are often
                updated, re-hosted, or taken down entirely; these links are current as
                of April 2026, but some may go dead over time.</p>
            <p><strong>HGIS and Spatial History Readings</strong></p>
            <ul class="incremental">
                <li><p>Bavaj, Lawson, and Struck. <em>Doing Spatial History</em>.
                        Routledge, 2021.</p></li>
                <li><p><a href="https://geospatialhistorian.wordpress.com/"><u>Clifford
                                and MacFayden.</u> <u><em>The Geospatial
                                    Historian</em>.</u></a></p></li>
                <li><p><a href="https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/staff/gregoryi/g2gp/ig-gis.pdf"><u>Gregory,
                                Ian.</u> <u><em>A Place in History: A Guide to Using GIS in Historical
                                    Research</em>. <sup>2</sup>2005.</u></a></p></li>
                <li><p>Gregory and Ell. <em>Historical GIS: Technologies, Methodologies,
                            and Scholarship.</em> CUP, 2007.</p></li>
                <li><p>Gregory and Geddes, eds. <em>Toward Spatial Humanities:
                            Historical GIS and Spatial History</em>. Indiana UP, 2014.</p></li>
                <li><p><a
                            href="http://www.geographicallyintegratedhistory.com/download/"
                        ><u>Owens
                                et al.</u> <u><em>A Geographic Information Systems (GIS) Training Manual
                                    for Historians and Historical Social Scientists</em>.</u></a></p></li>
                <li><p>Zhang and Logan. “Historical GIS.” <em>Oxford Bibliographies</em>
                        (OUP, 2017).</p></li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>HGIS Organizations, Communities, and Websites</strong></p>
            <ul class="incremental">
                <li><p><a href="http://www.hgis.org.uk/"><u>Historical GIS Research
                                Network</u></a></p></li>
                <li><p><a href="https://hgis.usask.ca/"><u>HGIS Lab, University of
                                Saskatchewan</u></a></p></li>
                <li><p><a
                            href="https://mappinghistory.uoregon.edu/english/index.html"
                        ><u>Mapping
                                History, University of Oregon</u></a></p></li>
                <li><p><a href="https://darmc.harvard.edu/"><u>Mapping Past Societies,
                                Harvard University.</u></a></p></li>
                <li><p><a
                            href="http://web.stanford.edu/group/spatialhistory/static/"
                        ><u>Spatial
                                History Project, Stanford.</u></a></p></li>
                <li><p><a href="https://whgazetteer.org/"><u>World Historical Gazetteer,
                                University of Pittsburgh</u></a></p></li>
            </ul>

            <blockquote>
                <p>Gabriel Moss, <a href="https://mossmaps.net/">MossMaps</a>, 2026. </p>
            </blockquote>

            <hr/>
            <ol>
                <li id="fn1"><p>These instructions assume that students will complete
                        the exercise on their own laptops (it works on PC, Mac, and Linux, so
                        technology barriers should be minimal).. Depending on the resources
                        available at your college or university, you may be able to host the
                        exercise in a computer lab with QGIS already installed.<a
                            href="#fnref1"
                            class="footnote-back"
                            role="doc-backlink"
                        >↩︎</a></p></li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
